{"text":"I've attached the source code for two repos. The first is for a Nix server that I'm setting up. Essentially, what these two things are supposed to be is a new deployment system that I wanted to try custom rolling. It's supposed to run on a VPS server that I host. And the idea is because it's all declared in a Nix config, as long as I update the images that it's pulling and rebuild the Nix image, I should just be able to automatically redeploy the system. And so if I'm adding like one or two projects a month, something like that, and it's like small self-contained Docker apps that really won't have many users, should be fine to just like easily deploy things. I'm now setting up a CLI. The purpose of the CLI is to automate some of the things I don't like doing. And so it will eventually include stuff like setting up a GitHub action that will just deploy onto my server without me having to think about it. Or just like with some basic UI prompts that will get me through, you know, dumping in, generating a new SSH key, whatever it is, any secrets management, boom, it just gets it done. And then maybe wrapping the GitHub CLI to like, oh, go do this as well. Like to like set up environment variables. For example, like the endpoint URL for the webhook would be something I can set up there. And so that's kind of what I'm planning for this tool. You can see what the CLI looks like. One of the limitations is that the CLI has to be on a machine where I've already checked out the repo. That's fine because I'm only really going to use it on my PC or my laptop, which both have my.files already checked out. But I wanted to get your input on the overall setup so far. What are some limitations? Are there security issues that I should be concerned about in doing this? Because I don't want to get fucked over and like have all my shit like attacked. I have the domain behind Cloudflare, so I have some DDoS protection. I guess one thing I definitely need to do is for the CLI, the webhook, probably add like rate limits so that you don't just like constantly rebuild the server and maybe like a queue for like a certain amount of rebuild requests. I don't know. What do you think?"}